version: 2
title: How a version-1 playbook works
description: The playbook feature as a playbook — each event is a moment the engine handles, its content the logic that runs then; what holds at every moment is under Always.
decisions:
  - key: host
    label: Does the host write back?
    values:
      - key: writes
        label: Yes — the desktop app, VS Code, the nodes preview
      - key: reads
        label: No — a read-only host
  - key: nesting
    label: Does any content entry name a .playbook?
    values:
      - key: 'no'
        label: 'No'
      - key: 'yes'
        label: 'Yes'
events:
  - key: always
    label: Always
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Always
    detail: What holds at every moment — the shape of the file, how content is chosen, the state everything is computed from, and where the engine lives.
    content:
      key: always
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What is always true
        description: The shape of the file, how content is chosen, the state everything is computed from, and where the engine lives.
        sections:
          - title: The file
            body: |
              A `.playbook` is a YAML document. The extension picks the engine
              (`packages/filekinds/src/lib/playbookDoc.ts`); the same engine renders it in the web
              Studio, the desktop app and the VS Code extension.

              Top-level keys, in the order a save writes them:

              | key | what it is |
              |---|---|
              | `version` | which playbook this is — absent means 1, the book this brief describes; `2` is events alone, one document each written in the book (see below) |
              | `title` | the heading; `Playbook` if missing |
              | `description` | one line under the title |
              | `view` | where the walk is — written by the host, see below |
              | `decisions` | the state space: questions with answers |
              | `events` | what can happen, and what each shows when taken |
              | `topics` | what is true while the assignment holds |
              | `rules` | how each event plays out under a condition |

              Parsing is lenient: a file the parser cannot read opens as an empty playbook, an unknown
              key is ignored, a missing `key` is slugged from the label. Nothing is generated while
              walking: everything drawn is in the book or, at version 1, in a file it names.
          - title: A ref — decision=answer
            body: |
              Every condition is a list of refs, each `decision=answer`. A list holds when EVERY ref in
              it is among the answers currently taken; an empty or absent list always holds.
              `when: [a=x, b=y]` means both, never either.
          - title: decisions
            body: |
              ```yaml
              decisions:
                - key: entity               # identity — never renumbered, never renamed in place
                  label: What are we?
                  detail: optional hover text
                  values:
                    - key: none
                      label: Nothing yet
                    - key: company
                      label: A company
                      activates: [banking]  # decisions that only exist once this answer is taken
                      when: [other=answer]  # this ANSWER is only on offer while the refs hold
              ```

              - A decision with no `activates` pointing at it is always on the table. One that some
                answer activates is on the table only while at least one of those answers is taken.
              - `when` on an answer gates the answer, not the question: the pill is not offered while
                the refs do not hold, and a taken answer that stops being offered is dropped.
              - A decision left with exactly one available answer is taken automatically and not shown.
                That answer drives every rule, topic and content choice but is NOT written to the file:
                `view.locks` stays the set somebody chose.
          - title: events
            body: |
              ```yaml
              events:
                - key: incorporate
                  label: We incorporate
                  trigger: chosen           # imposed (default) | chosen
                  arity: once               # once (default) | many
                  rate: 5/w                 # only meaningful with arity many
                  domain: Company           # a grouping heading in the rail, nothing more
                  detail: one italic line shown when the event is open
                  content:                  # what the event SHOWS when clicked (the version-1 form;
                    - file: incorporate.brief   #   version 2 writes the document here — see below)
                      label: optional panel header
                      by: [entity]          # optional — the file varies by these decisions
              ```

              - `trigger`: `imposed` arrives whether or not we are ready (a bolt); `chosen` fires when
                we say so (a hand). Imposed is the default.
              - `arity: many` never leaves the table (a repeat icon, `rate` beside the label); `once`
                is a move that belongs in an order.
              - `content` is what the event shows: any file kind the Studio renders — markdown, a brief,
                a guide, another playbook.
          - title: topics
            body: |
              ```yaml
              topics:
                - key: standing
                  label: Standing orders
                  when: [entity=company]    # optional — present only where this holds
                  content:
                    - file: standing.brief
              ```

              A topic is content that is live while its `when` holds; with no `when` it is live
              everywhere and the pane marks it `always`. Topics render in the right pane under whichever
              event is open; a lone topic with no open event takes the whole pane with no heading.
          - title: rules
            body: |
              ```yaml
              rules:
                - event: incorporate
                  when: [entity=company]    # optional condition over the assignment
                  status: n/a               # ready | gap | n/a
                  process: what we do — markdown
                  sets: [entity=company]    # answers this event changes — makes it a DOOR
                  note: free text
                  every: 12                 # months between recurrences, once it applies
                  from: incorporate         # the event whose completion anchors the clock
                  offset: 30                # days after the anchor before the first one is due
              ```

              - For one event, the FIRST rule in file order whose `when` holds wins.
              - `status`: `ready` (green) — the response is known · `gap` (red) — it can happen and
                there is no response · `n/a` — it cannot arise here; the event is hidden from the rail.
                An event with no matching rule is amber: "nobody has looked at this here."
              - `process` is the RESPONSE when there is no `sets`, and the PREREQUISITE when there is.
              - `sets` makes the event a door: taking it applies those answers and records the step in
                `view.history`.
              - `every` / `from` / `offset` describe recurrence for a plan to read; the walk ignores
                them.
          - title: content, and how an answer picks a document
            body: |
              Content is chosen by answers and never reads them. A plain entry is shown as is. An entry
              with `by: [d1, d2]` is a closed set: `variationOf(entry, locks)` takes the answer to each
              decision in `by`, in `by` order, and the segments `<d1=answer>,<d2=answer>` pick the
              member. Where the members live is what the version says.

              **Version 1 — files beside the book.** The entry is `{file, label?, by?}`; `file` is a
              base, and the member shown is

              ```
              <file>.variants/<d1=answer>,<d2=answer><same extension as file>
              ```

              `funding.playbook` by `entity` shows `funding.playbook.variants/entity=company.playbook`.
              The base file need not exist. Every `file` is relative to the folder of the ROOT playbook
              — the one the host opened — even when the entry sits in a nested book.

              Either way:

              - Every combination of the `by` decisions' answers is one whole document, authored up
                front; nothing is generated on open. While a `by` decision is unanswered the pane says
                "Answer <decision> to see this."
              - A member is handed nothing — no answers, no context. A playbook chosen this way walks
                on its own inside its panel.
              - Every decision in `by` must be a decision of the book that holds the entry.
          - title: Version 2 — written in the book, session-only
            body: |
              With `version: 2` at the top the book is decisions and events. On the event: `when` (on
              the table only where it holds; absent means always), `hint` (what to do before its
              document shows). The walk is
              SESSION-ONLY: every decision opens unanswered, and nothing clicked is ever written back.
              What is always true is the content of an always-on event.
              Each event shows ONE document in the book: `content` is one mapping, not a list;
              `kind` names the renderer, because there is no extension to pick it from; `doc` is the
              document as that kind's YAML parses it, a block string for `md`:

              ```yaml
              decisions:
                - key: entity
                  label: What are we?
                  values: [{key: company, label: A company}, {key: partnership, label: A partnership}]
              events:
                - key: incorporate
                  label: We incorporate
                  trigger: chosen
                  hint: Say what we are first.      # shown while entity is unanswered
                  content:
                    key: paperwork        # optional: what the walk collapses by
                    label: The paperwork  # optional: the panel header
                    kind: brief           # the kind of every member
                    by: [entity]          # the set follows this answer
                    docs:
                      entity=company: {title: As a company, sections: [{title: Reserve the name, body: Hold it for 60 days.}]}
                      entity=partnership: {title: As a partnership, sections: [{title: The deed, body: Sign it.}]}
                - key: open-bank
                  label: We open the account
                  trigger: chosen
                  when: [entity=company]            # on the table only as a company
                  content: {kind: md, doc: Take the certificate to the bank.}
              ```

              A `doc` is one document whatever the answers; a `by` + `docs` set is one document per
              combination of the `by` answers, keyed `decision=answer,...` in `by` order — the same
              segments a version-1 variant file name carries — so the document shown follows the
              answer. While a `by` decision is unanswered the pane shows the event's `hint` and asks
              for the answer; once it is taken the member for that answer shows. A nested book is
              `kind: playbook` with the whole book under `doc`, read at the version it says.
              Content has no path: it carries no annotations and is never pinned. A version-2
              book is ONE FILE — paste it, check it, render it with nothing else on disk; a document
              shared between books is a version-1 file.

              Refused: `doc` or `docs` without `kind`, `doc` and `docs` together, `by` on a `doc`, `docs`
              without `by`, a `docs` key that is not a combination of the `by` answers, a combination with
              no document, an `md` document that is not a string. Each version drops what only the other
              has and the checker names it: a version-1 file drops a written entry, and `when` or
              `hint` on an event (add `version: 2`, or move them to a rule); a version-2 file drops
              every entry of a `content` list but the first and a `file` on an entry (write the document
              into the book, or take `version: 2` off). A book that says `version: 2` writes that line
              back.

              `how-a-version-2-playbook-works.playbook`, beside this book, is the version-2 account,
              at version 2: one file, every document in it.
          - title: view — what the host writes
            body: |
              ```yaml
              view:
                locks: [entity=company, banking=open]   # the assignment — THE state
                history:                    # doors walked through, in order
                  - { event: incorporate, from: [entity=none], locks: [entity=company] }
                collapsed: [paperwork.brief]   # content panels folded shut
              ```

              Nothing else changes when the book is walked. A host that writes back (the nodes preview,
              the desktop app, VS Code) persists this block; a read-only host keeps it for the session.
              A version-2 book has no `view` at all: every host keeps its walk for the session.
          - title: The state
            body: |
              The state is the assignment — which answer is taken for each decision. It is never listed
              in the file. Which decisions are on the table, which events can arise, which rule
              applies, which topics are live and which file a `by` entry opens are computed from the
              assignment on every render.
          - title: The document engine
            body: |
              `packages/filekinds/src/lib/playbookDoc.ts` — types, `parsePlaybook`, `dumpPlaybook`,
              `legacyProblems`, `versionProblems`, `inlineProblems`, and the derivation: `meets`,
              `activeDecisions`, `impliedLocks`, `pruneLocks`, `take`, `applyRefs`, `ruleFor`,
              `topicsAt`, `eventsAt`, `variationOf`, `variationKeys`, `variationsOf`,
              `variationProblems`, `contentText`. Its header comment is
              what `studio-check --spec playbook` prints. `docVersion.ts` beside it is the versioning
              convention: `version: N` at the top, absent means 1, every version stays readable, a
              version the engine does not know is named.
          - title: The decision space
            body: |
              `packages/crosscut/src/decisions/decisionSpace.ts` — the hierarchy and predicates shared
              with `.guide` and `.memory`: `activates`, answer `when`, `toggleRef`, `pruneLocks`,
              `impliedLocks`, `decisionRows`. A playbook saves its assignment; a guide throws it away.
          - title: The views
            body: |
              - `packages/filekinds/src/components/playbook/PlaybookPreview.tsx` — the walk's host:
                persistence through the host's `onChange`, the trail's write and jump.
              - `PlaybookWalk.tsx` — the rail and the content pane, `ContentPanel`, `NestedPlaybook`,
                the lineage check, panes pushed into the enclosing `PaneTrail`.
              - `eventDetailRules.ts` — the decision table for what an open event's detail is.
              - `ElementView.tsx` — one decision or event alone, as a policy element.
              - `packages/crosscut/src/components/DecisionPills.tsx` and `EventRow.tsx` — the pills
                and the event row, presentation only.
          - title: The checker
            body: |
              `python/studio_kinds/kinds/playbook.py` — the `check`: parse, version, legacy keys, `by`
              decisions, the closed set of variations, written content (each document through its own
              kind's check, with the root file's path). The `studio-check` command, from the Python
              package.
  - key: opened
    label: The file is opened
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Opening
    detail: The extension picks the engine; parsePlaybook reads the YAML; the walk is drawn.
    content:
      key: opening
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: The file is opened
        description: The extension picks the engine, parsePlaybook reads the YAML, and the walk is drawn.
        sections:
          - title: Parse
            body: |
              `parsePlaybook(text)` reads the YAML once and never throws: an unreadable file opens as
              an empty playbook, an unknown key is ignored, `trigger` defaults to `imposed`, a missing
              `key` is slugged from its label. `version` absent means 1: a version-1 file drops any
              written-here entry; a version-2 file drops topics, the view, and any content entry that
              is not one written document. `view.locks` is pruned on the way in (`pruneLocks`): an answer to a
              decision that is not on the table is dropped before anything is drawn.
          - title: One view
            body: |
              The walk is the only view over the file. A book with no decisions, events or topics
              renders "An empty playbook — no decisions, events or topics yet." instead of a blank page.
              A host that writes back and hands over a version-1 file the engine cannot carry (a newer
              version, or version 1 holding written content) draws one line at the top: "Not saved — ….
              The walk is kept for this session only." A version-2 book is session-only by design and
              draws no line: every decision opens unanswered, and nothing clicked is written.
          - title: The walk
            body: |
              Two panes, a rail and a content pane, as one flat trail of panes.

              **The rail — "Where we are":**
              1. *How we got here*: the doors taken (`view.history`), each a pill; the last is where
                 the walk stands; `start` is before the first door. Drawn only once a door has been
                 taken.
              2. The decisions as pills (`decisionRows`), indented under the answer that opened them,
                 headed "Where we are · N of M answered". A decision down to one available answer is
                 hidden.
              3. The events that can arise (`eventsAt`), grouped under `domain` headings in the order
                 the domains first appear, imposed before chosen inside each group, with a search box.

              **The content pane:** the open event's `detail` line, then its content;
              a divider; then the live topics (`topicsAt`) headed "What is true here · N topics". One
              topic and no open event fills the pane alone. Before any event is clicked the pane says
              "Take an event to see what it shows."
  - key: changed-on-disk
    label: The file changes on disk
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Opening
    content:
      key: disk
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: The file changes on disk
        description: Open documents re-read themselves; the host's copy always wins over the preview's own.
        sections:
          - title: Who notices
            body: |
              - **VS Code**: reloads the document from disk when it changes and the Studio tab
                re-renders; its usual conflict handling applies if the tab has unsaved edits of its own.
              - **Desktop app**: watches the open folder; the open document re-reads itself when the
                file changes and no edit of its own is pending.
              - **Web Studio / Nodes**: work over the shared drive, not disk; a change on the drive is
                the signal.
          - title: Whose copy wins
            body: |
              When the host hands the preview new content, that content wins: the preview drops any
              session-only walk it was holding and parses the new text.
  - key: answered
    label: An answer is taken
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Walking
    detail: toggleRef, then pruneLocks — the whole assignment is recomputed, not one answer.
    content:
      key: answers
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: An answer is taken
        description: A pill click emits the COMPLETE next assignment — the answer applied or cleared, then every answer to a question that left the table pruned.
        sections:
          - title: One click, as a function
            body: |
              `toggleRef(decisions, locks, "entity=company")`:

              1. If the ref is already taken, clear it. Otherwise take it, replacing any other answer to
                 the same decision — a decision holds at most one answer.
              2. `pruneLocks` to a fixed point: drop every answer whose decision is no longer on the
                 table, and every answer that is no longer on offer (its `when` stopped holding) while
                 its question stayed. Repeat until nothing changes, because dropping one can strand
                 another.

              What comes out is the whole next `locks` list, never a delta.
          - title: What makes a decision appear
            body: |
              - **No enabler** — no answer anywhere names it in `activates` — always on the table.
              - **Enablers** — on the table while ANY of them is taken.
              - **Answer-level `when`** — an answer is offered only while its refs hold; while they do
                not, it is not an answer at all.
          - title: Implied answers
            body: |
              `impliedLocks`: a decision down to ONE available answer is taken automatically, iterated
              to a fixed point (taking one can activate another that is itself down to one), and never
              drawn. Implied answers drive rules, topics and `by` content, but are kept apart from
              `locks`, so what gets WRITTEN stays the set somebody chose.
          - title: How the rail lays them out
            body: |
              `decisionRows` walks the decisions in file order, depth-first through `activates`, and
              returns a flat list with a depth per row, so a child shows indented under the answer that
              opened it. A collapsed parent (one answer, hidden) adds no indent. A decision active
              through an answer that is not on screen is shown flat rather than dropped.
          - title: What is written
            body: |
              The new assignment goes into `view.locks` through the host's `onChange`; a host without
              one keeps it for the session. A pill click is not a door: the trail does not grow, and if
              the answers drift from the last door's the rail says "answers changed by hand since the
              last event."
  - key: event-clicked
    label: An event is clicked
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Walking
    detail: ruleFor picks the rule; the event-detail table picks the body.
    content:
      key: events
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: An event is clicked
        description: The rail lists what can arise; a click selects one and its content fills the pane. Which rule applies, what the row marks mean, what the pane shows, and what a door does.
        sections:
          - title: Which events are on the table
            body: |
              Events are global and unowned. `eventsAt` keeps every event whose current rule is not
              `n/a`, then sorts: by `domain` in the order the domains first appear in the file, and
              inside a domain `imposed` before `chosen`. A book with no domains is a flat list.
          - title: Which rule applies
            body: |
              `ruleFor(event)`: the first rule in file order whose `event` matches and whose `when` holds
              under the current answers, implied ones included. No matching rule is a legitimate
              outcome and is drawn as one.
          - title: The row
            body: |
              | mark | meaning |
              |---|---|
              | bolt | `trigger: imposed` — fires whether or not we are ready |
              | hand | `trigger: chosen` — fires when we say so |
              | repeat icon + rate | `arity: many` — never finished; capacity, not a step |
              | green dot | `status: ready` — the response is known |
              | red dot | `status: gap` — it can happen and there is no response |
              | amber dot, italic "nobody has looked at this here" | no rule matches |
              | not listed | `status: n/a` — it cannot arise here |
          - title: The detail pane
            body: |
              A click SELECTS the event: its content shows in the pane, never inline, so there is
              nothing to collapse; a second click only brings the pane back into view on a narrow
              screen. The pane stacks, top to bottom:

              1. The event's `detail` line, in italics.
              2. ONE kind of body, decided by the `event-detail` table (`eventDetailRules.ts`):

              | content entries | body |
              |---|---|
              | exactly one | that document, bare — no card, no header, filling the pane |
              | more than one | a card per entry with a header (its `label`, else "The sequence"), each collapsible |
              | none, and the rule has `process` | the process prose, rendered as markdown |
              | none, and no process | "No response recorded — this is a hole, not a nothing." |

              For a door with no prose the last line reads "No prerequisite recorded for this door."
              Collapsing a card is remembered in `view.collapsed` by the entry's `key`, else its file
              path (version 1), else its label.
          - title: Doors
            body: |
              A rule with `sets` makes the event a door. Under the open row a chip offers "Take it —"
              followed by the refs in `sets`. Clicking it applies every ref (each replacing any other
              answer to its decision), prunes, and — in the ROOT book — writes `locks` and appends
              `{event, from, locks}` to `view.history` in one save. A nested book just moves; its
              answers are never written. `process` on a door is what must be in hand BEFORE taking it.
          - title: The trail
            body: |
              "How we got here" lists the doors taken. Clicking an earlier pill jumps back to the
              assignment just after that door and TRUNCATES the trail there; `start` restores the
              assignment from before the first door and empties it. Only doors are recorded — a pill
              click is a hand edit, not a step.
  - key: nested-opened
    label: A nested book opens
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Walking
    when:
      - nesting=yes
    content:
      key: nesting
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: A nested book opens
        description: A .playbook as content walks on its own inside its panel. It is handed nothing, holds its own answers for the session, and refuses to contain itself.
        sections:
          - title: How it gets there
            body: |
              A content entry that is a playbook — at version 1 a file ending in `.playbook`, plain or
              picked by `by`; at version 2 an entry with `kind: playbook` and the book under `doc` —
              renders as a full walk instead of a document: its own rail, its own events, its own
              topics, inside the parent's content pane. Nesting has no depth limit.
          - title: Its answers
            body: |
              The nested book starts from ITS OWN `view.locks` (pruned) and holds them locally. They are
              never written back: the outer file owns the outer answers, and writing inner ones would mean
              two files disagreeing about who holds what. A door in a nested book just moves it — no
              trail entry, no save.
          - title: What crosses
            body: |
              Nothing. The parent's answers chose the book; the child does not read them, does not see a
              library, does not receive context. A child that should differ by a parent answer is a
              different FILE, picked by `by` — or, at version 2, a different `docs` member.
          - title: Paths
            body: |
              Every `file` inside a version-1 child is still resolved against the ROOT book's folder —
              the folder the Studio opened. A child in a sub-folder refers to its neighbours by the path
              from the root, not from itself. A written child has no path of its own; a version-1 book
              written inside a version-2 one still names files beside the root.
          - title: Cycles
            body: |
              The chain of books open above is carried down. A file already on that chain is refused
              with "`<file>` is already open above — a book cannot contain itself", because expanding it
              again would never end. That is the one thing refused; any other nesting is allowed.
          - title: Panes
            body: |
              The child's detail and drill panes join the enclosing trail as further panes to the right,
              so however deep the nesting, the screen is one flat chain with one scroll. Pane keys carry
              the book they belong to, so an inner event with the same key as an outer one does not
              collide.
  - key: saved
    label: The Studio saves it
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Walking
    detail: Parse, then dump — the file is re-emitted from the parsed document.
    when:
      - host=writes
    content:
      key: saving
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: The Studio saves it
        description: A save is parse then dump. The file is re-emitted from the parsed document, so what the parser did not keep is gone.
        sections:
          - title: What triggers one
            body: |
              Any change to the view in a host that writes back: a pill click, a door taken, a content
              panel collapsed. The desktop app, VS Code's Studio editor and the nodes preview write; a
              read-only host holds the change for the session and drops it.
          - title: What comes out
            body: |
              `dumpPlaybook` writes, in this order: `version` (from 2 up; a version-1 file gets no
              line), `title`, `description` (if any), `view` (if non-empty), `decisions`, `events`,
              `topics` (omitted when empty), `rules` (omitted when empty). Inside each item, keys come
              out in the engine's order with defaults dropped: `trigger` is always written, `arity` only
              when `many`, `by` only when non-empty, and so on. Long lines are not wrapped.
          - title: What is lost
            body: |
              - **Comments** — a save is a parse and a dump; YAML comments do not survive.
              - **Unknown keys** — anything the parser did not recognise.
              - **Formatting** — quoting style, blank lines, flow-vs-block sequences.
          - title: What stays put
            body: |
              Keys are identity. A save never renames or reorders a decision, answer or event, and
              never renumbers anything. A key renamed by hand leaves every `when`, `sets`, `by`,
              `view.locks`, `view.history` and variant file name that used the old key pointing at
              nothing, and nothing warns.
          - title: What is held, and never written
            body: |
              A version-1 file this engine cannot carry is not saved at all: a `version` newer than the
              engine knows, or a version-1 file holding content written in the book. A save re-emits
              what was read — the newer file would come back as version 2 with its newer keys gone, the
              version-1 file without its written entry. So the walk is kept for the session, as a
              read-only host keeps it, and a line at the top says `Not saved — …`.

              A version-2 book is never saved from the walk: it has no `view`, and answers, doors and
              folded panels live in the host's memory for the session. The file changes only when its
              author edits it.
          - title: What only the walk changes
            body: |
              `view.locks`, `view.history`, `view.collapsed`. Nothing under `decisions`, `events`,
              `topics` or `rules` changes from walking — the Studio has no authoring surface for a
              playbook. A nested book's answers are never written anywhere.
  - key: authored
    label: A book is written
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Authoring
    content:
      key: authoring
      kind: guide
      doc:
        title: Write a playbook
        description: From the template to a file the checker passes.
        decisions:
          - key: varies
            label: Does any content differ by an answer?
            values:
              - key: same
                label: No, every entry is one file
              - key: varies
                label: Yes, some entries vary by answer
        steps:
          - key: template
            label: Start from the template
            detail: |
              `studio-check --template playbook > new.playbook`; `studio-check --spec playbook` prints
              the engine's account of every field. A field the engine does not know is dropped on the
              next save.
          - key: decisions
            label: Write the decisions
            after:
              - template
            detail: |
              One decision per question whose answer changes what is on the table. Keys are identity:
              they appear in every `when`, `sets`, `by` and variant file name. A question that exists
              only under an answer goes in that answer's `activates`; an answer offered only under
              another gets its own `when`.
          - key: events
            label: Write the events
            after:
              - template
            detail: |
              `trigger: chosen` when we decide it happens, `imposed` when the world does. `domain`
              groups them in the rail. `arity: many` for something that recurs and never finishes.
          - key: rules
            label: Write the rules
            after:
              - events
              - decisions
            detail: |
              For each event, the conditions that change how it plays out, specific first and the plain
              `status: ready` last. `n/a` where it cannot arise. `sets` where taking it changes an
              answer. `process` for the response when there is no file to attach.
          - key: content
            label: Attach content
            after:
              - events
            detail: |
              `content:` on an event is what it shows; on a topic (`when?`) what is true while the
              assignment holds. Any Studio kind renders. The version line picks the form, and a book has
              one form only: no `version:` line, and every entry is a file beside the book —
              `{file, label?, by?}`, paths relative to the root book's folder; `version: 2`, and every
              event has ONE document written in — `content: {kind, doc}` — no topics, no `file`, no
              `view`: the walk is session-only.
          - key: variants
            label: Author the variant files
            when:
              - varies=varies
            after:
              - content
            detail: |
              `{file: x.brief, by: [d]}` and a folder `x.brief.variants/` holding `d=<answer>.brief` for
              EVERY answer of `d`. Each is a whole document that reads nothing from the parent. (At
              version 2: `{kind: brief, by: [d], docs: {d=<answer>: …}}`, a member per answer, and
              `hint` on the event saying what to do before answering.)
          - key: check
            label: Check it
            after:
              - rules
              - content
            detail: |
              `studio-check new.playbook` — exit 1 lists legacy keys, `by` decisions that do not exist,
              missing variant files, refused written entries, and an entry on the wrong side of the
              version line.
  - key: checked
    label: studio-check runs on it
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Authoring
    content:
      key: checker
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: studio-check runs on it
        description: What the checker verifies for a .playbook, what it prints, and what it does not catch.
        sections:
          - title: What it checks
            body: |
              `studio-check <file>` (or a folder) picks the engine by extension and runs, in order:

              1. **Parse** — a YAML error is the only problem reported; nothing else is tried.
              2. **Legacy keys** — `library:`, `compare:`, topic `variants:`, `view.against`,
                 `view.tab`, `materials:`, `scales:`, `view.thresholds`. Each names what replaced it.
              3. **`by` names a decision** — every decision in every `by` must be one of the book's.
              4. **The closed set** — in a version-1 book, for every `by` entry, every combination of
                 its decisions' answers must be a file beside the book: `<file>.variants/<d=a,...>.<ext>`.
                 Each missing one is a problem.
              5. **The version** — `version:` must be a whole number the engine knows (up to 2). Each
                 version drops what only the other has, and the checker names it: a version-1 file
                 holding content written in the book is told to add `version: 2`; a version-2 file is
                 told about `topics`, a `view`, a `content` list, a `file`, `by` or `docs` on an entry
                 — write the document into the book, fold the cases into one brief, delete the view, or
                 take `version: 2` off.
              6. **Content written in the book** — `kind` must be a kind the Studio knows; an `md`
                 document is a string. Then each written document is checked by its own kind's engine,
                 handed the root file's path, so a written version-1 book's own files resolve beside the
                 root — where the walk reads them.

              The summary line reads `vN · N decisions, N events, N topics` and, when any `by` file
              entry exists, `k of m child variations present`, and `n written inline` when content is
              written in the book. Any problem is exit 1 — the same judgement the Studio makes.
          - title: What it does not check
            body: |
              - That a plain version-1 `file` entry exists. A missing file shows in the pane as
                `<file> — <error>` when opened; the checker does not read it.
              - That refs in `when`, `sets` or `view.locks` name real decisions and answers. A stale ref
                never holds (or is pruned on open) — it fails quietly, not loudly.
              - That a rule's `event` names an existing event. Such a rule is inert.
              - The content files themselves; the checker run on the folder covers them.
              - Whether a host may save the file: that is the host's own hold, judged from the same
                version problems this checker prints.
          - title: Running it
            body: |
              `studio-check --kinds` lists the kinds; `--spec playbook` prints the engine's header and
              template; `--template playbook` prints a fresh file.
